{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear.",
      "reason": "The abstract background describes the current state of knowledge (increasing reinfection reports) and explicitly identifies the key uncertainty (unclear trend of reinfection rate over time); the Introduction (text_4) further describes the global burden and ongoing mutation of SARS-CoV-2."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "The results of this study could help guide public health measures and vaccination strategies in response to the Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic.",
      "reason": "The importance and need for the review are explained: the unclear time trend of reinfection motivates the meta-analysis, and results are framed as guiding public health measures and vaccination strategies."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "Although the Discussion mentions advantages 'compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate', no previous review is identified or cited in the Introduction, and the retrieved evidence does not show the need for the current review being justified relative to a cited prior review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a review of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates (prognostic/prevalence evidence), not a review of intervention effects, so no mechanism of action needs to be described."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review does not evaluate complex interventions or delivery contexts, so a logic model is not applicable."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 82
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The objectives are explicitly stated: to summarize current SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates across regions/populations and to examine time trends in reinfection rates. The objectives are clearly articulated, though not framed in a formal question framework; the aims are explicit and complete."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "This is a review of reinfection rates (frequency estimation) with meta-regression, not a review of intervention effects, so planned PICO comparisons do not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection... The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly specifies eligibility criteria tied to the review question (estimating SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rate), eligible study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive/cross-sectional), a working definition of reinfection with a minimum 90-day interval between positive tests, and exclusion criteria including minimum sample size, prohibited study types, duplicate populations, and grey literature."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language... (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "Language criteria (no language limit, with translation procedures) and publication status (original research only; grey literature excluded) are reported, but no restriction or criterion regarding dissemination year/publication date is specified."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": "text_8",
      "quote": "One of them reported only the reinfection rate, but not the associated number of first infections as well as the number of reinfections... Therefore, we excluded the above literature with the number of unreported infections",
      "reason": "The review reports excluding a study because required numbers were not reported, but it does not explicitly distinguish exclusion because outcomes were not measured from exclusion because outcome results were not reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed by meta-regression to fit stratified rates of reinfection based on variables such as variant and country, and we also used spline regression to fit time-varying reinfection rates that did not account for heterogeneity. In addition, considering the change in the reinfection rate over time, we constructed a multiple meta-regression using the time interval between two positive tests as the independent variable, to estimate the time-varying reinfection rate.",
      "reason": "The synthesis groups are specified (subgroups by age, variant, country, study population, and study type per Table 1 and text_10) and are linked to the review objectives of summarizing reinfection rates across regions/populations and examining trends over time via meta-regression on the time interval between infections."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_19",
      "quote": "In order to avoid data heterogeneity in metaanalysis while ensuring data quantity, we used previous literature and previous definitions of reinfection in some countries as the criteria for included literature and data extraction in this study.",
      "reason": "Rationales are provided for some notable restrictions, e.g., the 90-day reinfection definition is based on WHO and US CDC guidance (text_7) and was chosen to avoid heterogeneity while ensuring data quantity (text_19), and grey literature exclusion is acknowledged in the limitations; however, no rationale is given for other restrictions such as the minimum sample size of 20."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 88
}

